Native-host relocation capture
=============================

The runner is a one-object-per-process capture client for the native linker.
It is currently built for the arm64 Darwin host and Mach-O fixtures. The
input snapshot and command-line handling live in `runner.c`; host callbacks
and phase snapshots live in `runner_host.c`; the JSON writer is in
`runner_json.c`; and `runner_internal.h` shares their capture state. The
runner links through installed `liblink` headers and the native archive with
the literal `-llink` name. It contains no expected relocation formulas.

Compile and link with the configured host C compiler, installed liblink
headers, the source tree needed for the private Mach-O metadata structure, and
the directory containing `liblink.a`:

    for source in runner runner_host runner_json; do
      "$CC" -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror=vla -pedantic \
        -isystem "$LIBLINK_INCLUDE" -I liblink/src \
        -c "liblink/tests/runner/$source.c" -o "$source.o"
    done
    "$CC" -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror=vla -pedantic \
      runner.o runner_host.o runner_json.o \
      -L"$LIBLINK_LIBRARY_DIR" -llink -o liblink-runner

Run one object per process:

    ./liblink-runner liblink/tests/fixtures/generated/macho-arm64/ARM64_RELOC_UNSIGNED.o

The JSON contains the original object bytes, section addresses and raw
relocation records, pre-resolution section bytes, implicit field bytes,
explicit ADDEND records and their pairing, observed external symbol addresses,
GOT and stub layout, and bytes captured after relocation but before
initialization. It also records the host-provided symbol map and object-image
base. Each external relocation keeps the backend's observed `S` apart from a
host-map or image-base input when one exists. Symbols supplied by the object
are left for the independent oracle to derive from the retained object bytes.

The `A` value preserves its object-file representation; an independent oracle
must decode the mathematical addend. `pre_resolve_bytes` are copied in the
object registration callback. Relocated section, GOT, and stub bytes are copied
by the before-initialization hook. `post_init_live_bytes` are read after
resolution returns, so a fixture can show how an initializer changed already
relocated memory. `events_after_resolve` records initializer callbacks before
that read. Finalizers run only after JSON emission.

This slice exercises native arm64 Darwin Mach-O only. Unsupported relocations
terminate through the host fatal callback and must run in separate processes.
The runner does not claim ELF, COFF, or other host-backend coverage.

ELF x86_64 and AArch64 captures
------------------------------

elf_capture.c is a separate private client for a 64-bit POSIX host with the
x86_64 or AArch64 ELF target/backend compiled in. It captures
an unregistered object's section and extras bytes immediately before and
after the actual backend resolve operation. It never enters the initializer
pass or calls loaded code. Without --noexec it checks native addresses on an
x86_64 host. With --noexec it configures private NO_EXEC|ALL_FORMATS at the
explicit base0x10000000 and captures heap work/synthetic execution addresses.
This capture does not exercise the full core lifecycle; noexec_core.c does.

For AArch64 normal-address baselines, build both the library and this client
with -DLL_TARGET_ARCH=LL_ARCH_AARCH64 and -DLL_ALL_BACKENDS. The current data-only
baseline runs on an x64 POSIX host; the separate ARM64 native extras-verifier
repair remains pending. AArch64 captures include section-adjacent stub storage
before/after resolution and the final stub identity/order/targets. --far-function
forces branch veneers. --negative-data puts the independent resolver input in
the preceding page for PREL32/ADRP negative controls. Ordinary AArch64 resolver
inputs derive from the chosen section layout and retain a nonzero aligned low
offset, so narrow and scaled fields have useful in-range values. No instruction
is executed, and AArch64 NO_EXEC remains unsupported by the library.

    "$CC" -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Werror=vla -pedantic -DLL_ASSERTS \
      -DLL_ALL_BACKENDS -isystem "$LIBLINK_CONFIG_INCLUDE" \
      -isystem "$RTS_FS_INCLUDE" -I liblink/include -I liblink/src \
      liblink/tests/runner/elf_capture.c -L"$LIBLINK_LIBRARY_DIR" \
      -llink $RTS_FS_LINK_FLAGS -pthread -o elf_capture
    ./elf_capture liblink/tests/fixtures/generated/elf-x86_64/x86_64-linux-gnu/R_X86_64_64.o
    ./elf_capture --noexec liblink/tests/fixtures/generated/elf-x86_64/x86_64-linux-gnu/R_X86_64_64.o

RTS_FS_LINK_FLAGS supplies the installed rts-fs archive on Linux and is empty
on Darwin. Add the same sanitizer flags as the library when it is instrumented.
The capture uses always_pic, as native Darwin does. A non-PIC fixture requesting
addresses below4GiB collides with a normal Darwin executable's __PAGEZERO;
this capture does not change the linker's placement policy.

external_data is the fixed representable resolver input0x12345000. Ordinary
external_function inputs are chosen after placement, within range of the
loaded image. --far-function instead supplies0x700000000000 to force the PLT32
island. Both inputs are recorded separately from relocated bytes; they are
never called or dereferenced. The independent ELF oracle checks the formulas,
payloads and every untouched byte. No foreign Windows execution is claimed.
